# Git: no auto-push

Do **not** auto-commit or auto-push after ordinary coding.

- **Commit** only when the user asks to commit, or when they say **push to main** (commit first if needed, then push).
- **Push** only when the user explicitly says **push to main** (or the same meaning: push this work onto `origin main`).
- Before a push to `main`, **ask permission in chat** if they have not already said **push to main** in this turn. If they just said **push to main**, that is the permission — then push.
- Never force-push to main/master unless they explicitly demand it.
- Never commit secrets (`.env`, credentials, PATs, private keys).
- Skip empty commits. If there is nothing to commit, do not push.
- Use HTTPS GitHub auth already configured on this machine. Do **not** embed PATs in remotes, commits, or chat.
